[ITA_AFR.Description]
[ITA_AFR.FullName]
"Centauro"

[ITA_AFR.Epilogue]
After performing an offensive operation in the El Guettar area, the Axis forces were successfully repelling poorly coordinated attacks of the English and American forces up to 6 April 1943. However, on April 12, the tank units of the Centauro Division were united with the 15th German Tank Division. The united divisional battle group, supported by the Bersaglieri, took positions near Takruna. On April 26, the last 10 М14/41 tanks were assigned to the 10th German Tank Division and the Centauro Division was officially disbanded. But the history of Centauro was not over, as later in Italy the 136th Tank Division was formed, receiving its name - the 136th Legionary Armored Division Centauro (136. Divisione Corazzata Legionaria Centauro) - right before the capitulation of Italy in September 1943. In September, the division trained near Rome and fought the Germans that occupied Italy as part of the motorized corps (Corpo d'Armata Motocorazzato (CAM)).

[Agheila.Description]
\t On December13, the Centauro battle group bringing up the rear of the mobile Italian and German units was attacked by the 8th British Tank Brigade. The Italian field artillery and tanks locked in battle with more than 80 British tanks. The Italians were aptly using the anti-tank gun in ambush to stop the enemy strike and lost 6 tanks and 12 guns. 50 men were captured prisoners. The British lost more than 40 tanks and a few armored cars.
[Agheila.FullName]
The road to El Agheila
[Sened.Description]
\t On 30 January 1943, the 1st American Tank Division attacked the Italian positions near Maknassy. The Centauro division was holding grimly, combat action took all day. The Americans, suffering heavy losses, managed to capture the Es Sened station on February 1.
[Sened.FullName]
At Es Sened station
[Maknassy.Description]
\t The American tanks reached Maknassy, but on 2 February Italian and German units performed several counterattacks. A few American units fled in panic, leaving their heavy weapons behind. The advance of the Axis forces was only stopped by air strikes of the Allied aircraft.
[Maknassy.FullName]
The outskirts of Maknassy
[Kasserine_IT.Description]
\t In the morning of 20 February, the Afrika Korps performed a major strike in the direction of the Kasserine pass. The 5th Bersaglieri Regiment was assigned to the right flank. The Italians were supported by German guns. The units of the 15th Tank Division and the Centauro tank battalion remained in reserve.
\t The Bersaglieri were advancing under heavy artillery and mortar fire; nevertheless, they successfully captured Djebel Semama after a hard-fought battle.
[Kasserine_IT.FullName]
Sirocco above the Kasserine
[Elhamra.Description]
\t On 21 February, Rommel sent a reconnaissance unit, including a small M14 tank unit from the Centauro Division, to the north-west of the Kasserine Pass. The Italians spotted the positions of the 1st American Tank Division that was preparing to counterattack.
 \t At 14.00 hours, the units of the 15th Tank Division and Centauro Division occupied Djebel el Hamra and prepared to repel the enemy attack. The fighting went deep into the night. In the evening, a group of Semovente SPG arrived to support the infantry and ebentually the American attacks were repelled.
Heavy resistance occurred on 23 February, too.
[Elhamra.FullName]
The Rocks Of El Hamra
[DjBerda.Description]
\t After a final breach of the Mareth Line, the units of the 2nd American Corps attempted to cut off the Italian and German forces that were withdrawing along the coastline. The Americans were met by the defending 10th German Tank Division near Djebel el Mhaylat and Centauro units with the Imperiali Special Brigade near Djebel Berda.
\t Due to the lack of forces, the defending party used an interconnected chain of fortified strong points, armed with antitank weapons and located favorably, than a single line of defense. Behind the fortified points, mobile armored groups were operating, with a task to eliminate all possible breaches by the enemy.
[DjBerda.FullName]
Djebel Berda
[Guettar.Description]
\t On 3 April, the units of the Italian Centauro Tank Division and Superga Infantry Division performed a local offensive operation to the east of El Guettar, in an attempt to draw the attention of the Allied forces away from the coastal frontline. After intense battles, they successfully forced the Americans back around 10 km to the west. The Axis forces successfully repelled poorly coordinated attacks of the British and American forces up to 6 April 1943.
[Guettar.FullName]
Diversionary attack
[Hill141.Description]
\t Hill 141 was a critical position in the Djebel Srafi sector, in the line of defense near Enfidaville. The high ground positions were held by an infantry battalion, supported by several antitank guns and machine guns, and hill 141 itself was protected by the 2nd Company under command of captain Raumi.
\t On 25 April, the British Commonwealth forces suddenly attacked the positions. The attack was repelled, then after a hellish 45-minute artillery strike the enemy troops attacked again, supported by heavy mortar fire. The high ground changed hands a few times, but the Italians managed to drive the British out with a bayonet assault.
[Hill141.FullName]
Hill 141
